A. JavaScript Date Output
By default, JS will use the browser's time zone and display a date as a full text string
Tue Apr 15 2025 12:18:34 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)

B. Creating Date Objects
Date objects are created with the new Date() constructor.
There are 9 ways to create a new date object:

new Date()
new Date(date string)
new Date(year,month)
new Date(year,month,day)
new Date(year,month,day,hours)
new Date(year,month,day,hours,minutes)
new Date(year,month,day,hours,minutes,seconds)
new Date(year,month,day,hours,minutes,seconds,ms)
new Date(milliseconds)

new Date() creates a date object with the current date and time

new Date(date string) creates a date object from a date string
const d = new Date("October 13, 2014 11:13:00"); //output Mon Oct 13 2014 11:13:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)
const d = new Date("2022-03-25"); //output Fri Mar 25 2022 05:45:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)

new Date(year,month,...) creates a date object with a specified date and time
7 numbers specify year, month, day, hour, minute, second and millisecond (in that order)

const d = new Date(2018, 11, 24, 10, 33, 30, 0); //output Mon Dec 24 2018 10:33:30 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)
JS counts months from 0 to 11, January=0, December=11
Specifying a month higher than 11 will not result in an error but add the overflow to the next year
const d = new Date(2018, 15, 24, 10, 33, 30); is same as new Date(2019, 3, 24, 10, 33, 30);

upto 2 numbers specify year and month
const d = new Date(2018, 11); //Sat Dec 01 2018 00:00:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)
You can not omit month. If you supply only one parameter it will be treated as milliseconds
const d = new Date(2018); //output Thu Jan 01 1970 05:30:02 GMT+0530 (Nepal Time)

C. Previous Century
One and two digits years will be interpreted as 19xx
new Date(99, 11, 24); //output Fri Dec 24 1999 00:00:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)
new Date(9, 11, 24); //output Fri Dec 24 1909 00:00:00 GMT+0541 (Nepal Time)

D. JS stores dates as milliseconds since January 01, 1970
new Date(milliseconds) creates a new date object as milliseconds plus zero time
new Date(100000000000); 01 January 1970 plus 100 000 000 000 milliseconds

E. Displaying Dates
JS will (by default) output dates using the toString()  method. This is a string representation of the date, including the
time zone. The format is specified in the ECMAScript specification
Tue Apr 15 2025 12:18:34 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)
When you display a date object in HTML, it is automatically converted to a string with the toString() method.
The toDateString() method converts a date to a more readable format

const d = new Date();
d.toDateString(); //output Tue Apr 15 2025

The toUTCString() method converts a date to a sting using the UTC standard
const d = new Date();
d.toUTCString(); //output Tue, 15 Apr 2025 06:55:31 GMT

The toISOString() method converts a date to a string using the ISO format

const d = new Date();
d.toISOString();

2025-04-15T06:56:27.340Z

F. JS Date Input
There are generally 3 types of JS date input formats
ISO Date:"2015-03-25" (The International Standard)
Short Date:"03/25/2015"
Long Date:"Mar 25 2015" or "25 Mar 2015"

G. Independent of input format, JS will by default output dates in full text string format
Tue Apr 15 2025 12:42:06 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)

H. JS ISO Dates
Complete Date: new Date("2015-03-25");
Year and Month: new Date("2015-03");
Only Year: new Date("2015");
Date-Time:ISO dates can be written with added hours, minutes, and seconds (YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ):
new Date("2015-03-25T12:00:00Z");
Date and Time is seperated with a capital T
UTC time is defined with a capital letter Z

I. JS Get date methods
In JS date objects are created with new Date(), which will returns a date object with the current date and time

getFullYear()	Get year as a four digit number (yyyy)
getMonth()	Get month as a number (0-11)
getDate()	Get day as a number (1-31)
getDay()	Get weekday as a number (0-6)
getHours()	Get hour (0-23)
getMinutes()	Get minute (0-59)
getSeconds()	Get second (0-59)
getMilliseconds()	Get millisecond (0-999)
getTime()	Get time (milliseconds since January 1, 1970)

To return month or day as name we can use array
const months = ["January", "February", "March", "April", "May", "June", "July", "August", "September", "October", "November", "December"];

const d = new Date("2021-03-25");
let month = months[d.getMonth()]; //Output March

const days = ["Sunday", "Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday", "Saturday"];

const d = new Date("2021-03-25");
let day = days[d.getDay()]; //output Thursday

The Date.now() returns the number of milliseconds since January 1, 1970

J. Set Date Methods

Set Date methods let you set date values (years, months, days, hours,  minutes, seconds, milliseconds) for a Date Object

setDate()	Set the day as a number (1-31)
setFullYear()	Set the year (yyyy)
setHours()	Set the hour (0-23)
setMilliseconds()	Set the milliseconds (0-999)
setMinutes()	Set the minutes (0-59)
setMonth()	Set the month (0-11)
setSeconds()	Set the seconds (0-59)
setTime()	Set the time (milliseconds since January 1, 1970)

const d = new Date("January 01, 2025");
d.setFullYear(2020); //output Wed Jan 01 2020 00:00:00 GMT+0545 (Nepal Time)

